    {
      "knowledge_point": {
        "db_id": 17592186068918,
        "id": "fc24eade-1b3d-5672-88fd-0bb48b371509",
        "title": "Integrating Quotients Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "6365eecd-51bd-5d2b-b4cb-0671b340a575",
        ":question/problem": "Use the substitution $u=\\sec{\\left(x \\right)} + 1$. Which integral is obtained?\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{3 \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sec{\\left(x \\right)} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=\\sec{\\left(x \\right)} + 1$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{3 \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} \\sec{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sec{\\left(x \\right)} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=3\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n3\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
